A||||14||Spektyr||Yup, more questions...||||5||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-21-2001||01:26 AM||||Okay... been driving my car for a couple days now (the Lincoln is starting to get jealous.  I only go to it to get my tools out of the trunk.)  I've run into some minor concerns/problems I want to get some advice on.<P>1.  Shifter confidence.  The gearshift is not very solid going in and out of gears.  The forward throw gears (1st & 3rd) especially are hard to get in, and difficult to 'feel' when they are in.  More than a few times I just rev up when the light turns green and then frantically struggle to get it in first, or get that lovely grinding noise when I don't get 3rd in right.  It seems like when I push it in gear it has a 'hard spot' right before it actually engages, so it feels like I've reached the end of the throw, but I haven't.  I'm planning to go through the Haynes manual shifter adjustment, but I'm not ready to stop driving it just yet.<P>2. Braking effort:  It requires a lot of pedal pressure to stop the car quickly, and my Lincoln seems to have better stopping distance.  This could be just a perception difference from sitting a foot and a half higher off the ground, but I definitely have to stand on the pedal to get the tires to lock on the Fiero.  It's not spongy or uneven, just takes a lot of pressure.  Aftermarket brakes are on my list of things to buy soon, so I'm not particularly worried about this at the moment.<P>3. Air filter box: Now this scares me.  The previous owner told me a story today and swore that the Fiero floats.  (He ran through a Kansas-sized puddle, the streets here can fill up with thigh-high water in some places in heavy rain.)  Then I go to put in my K&N filter and find rust and debris inside the air filter box, and rust residue leading into the air inlet hose.  I cleaned it out pretty well, installed the new filter and put everything back together.  The engine runs pretty good, I'm just not sure how it survived this.  If there was going to be a problem from this, it would've manifested by now, right?  (the swimming Fiero fiasco was last year)<P>Thanks for the input.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Spektyr||04-21-2001||02:55 AM||||Okay, I just read the shifter adjustment instructions.  Haynes makes the assumption you know what all the parts of the shifter assembly are called.  Step 5 says: With the shifter lever in first gear insert alignment pins into the alignment holes.<P>Um... huh?  I read I can use 5/32 or No. 22 drill bits as alignment pins, but what holes are they talking about?<P>Step 6 Remove lash from the transaxle by rotating the vertical lever back while tightening the cable nut.<P>Here I'm assuming that 'lash' refers to an unwanted condition, not an actual part, but I'm not entirely sure what that condition is.<P>Can someone clear this up for me?  Seems like a pretty simple job once I figure out what they want me to do.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||04-21-2001||07:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR><B>Um... huh?  I read I can use 5/32 or No. 22 drill bits as alignment pins, but what holes are they talking about?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>These would be holes that will "freeze" the shifter in a known position prior to adjustment. The shifter cable hole is on the left side of the lever, just below and forward of the pivot. The holes should line up in the 1st gear position. The select holes are in the select lever just to the right of where the cable attaches.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Step 6 Remove lash from the transaxle by rotating the vertical lever back while tightening the cable nut.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Lash = slop, slackness. They are suggesting that once you have the shifter locked in place that you make sure the slack out of the other end before you tighten things down. For example, when you lock the shifter in 1st (cable extended at the trans) and go to the trans to set it, pull the lever out just a little more before locking the adjustment. That way you are assured that when the cable reaches that point it will be firmly in 1st(3rd or 5th too) and not just almost there (grind!) Have fun!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektyr||04-21-2001||08:03 AM||||Thanks, as soon as the sun comes up enough I think I'll head outside and do this.<P>Oh, and add another question:  Can someone tell me the difference between the transmissions that were made for the Fiero?  I've looked all over, but found only limited reference to final ratio's, etc.  If my math is correct, the 4 speed can't exceed 140mph without going over 6000rpm.  I don't NEED to go that fast, but I'd like to be able to someday.  You understand, it isn't about how fast you go, it's about how fast you could go, if you wanted to.<P>__________<BR>UPDATE:  Did the shifter adjustment.  It's better but not perfect.  Worth the 30min or so it took to tinker with it, as it's much more difficult to half-mesh the synchros.  I'll probably end up getting used to it before I get time to tear it apart again... work week approaching and I tend to sleep on all non-work hours.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<P>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-21-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-22-2001||12:42 AM||||Bump.||||reg||14
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||04-22-2001||01:36 AM||||Manual trannys for the Fiero were never, NEVER, EVER accused of being butter smooth. The shifting clunck, clunk, is better in some Fieros worse in other Fieros. If you are looking for a tranny that is say as smooth shifting as a Honda ("snick, snick" I believe is how its been described) you have chosen the wrong car. As I understand it, you hit the synchros before you actually hit the gear.<P>However, an interesting fact that I was told was the Ferraris of the same time frame as the Fiero were just as bad. But the clunk, clunk was treated as an "idiosynchrosy" rather than a sloppy tranny. I think we have all gotten used to it in different ways. I always sink it into second before pushing it into first just so I know I got first and not reverse. (85 GT 4 spd) <P>For the third shift grind, for me it did that only if I was trying to shift too fast and didn't move the shifter throught the "neutral" area. It seems to really hate when the driver tries to dive into 3rd in the wrong angle. After a while, its like having a girlfriend, you know what to do to make her sing and you know what not to do to piss her off.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-22-2001||01:35 PM||||LOL.  Yup, know what you mean.<P>Only revved it once at a light (missed 1st) and ground 3rd once today.  I'm getting the hang of the shifter, and both times I was in a hurry (read, having too much fun).<P>I'll tell you one thing though... just the tiniest dampness on the pavement and she'll put her ass all over the place... just scary enough to be fun without really being dangerous.<P>Thanks for the advice, keep it coming.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tryxalon||04-22-2001||03:54 PM||||If you are used to a full syncromesh Euro or Japanese style direct-link shifter the "american style" Fiero shifter operated by cable is definately different!!! After my Rx7's, X1/9 etc it was a definate change!! <P>The Fiero 4 and 5 speeds (even the one designed by Getrag and the one made by Isuzu) have the feel of the old Muncie "rock-crusher" transmissions: quite strong, but the "gate" is too "long" to be speed shifted or slammed. Yep, just like the 70's Ferrari's, Lambos and even the 4speed in the Vette. It will not just "snick" into place, you have to "put" it there, and then let out on the clutch - almost a non-syncro kind of shift. <P>Ya get used to it. Just like you get used to driving to maximize the use of torque rather than horsepower if you're driving an Iron Duke. <P>||||reg||6
Z||000008||Tryxalon||04-22-2001||04:02 PM||||Find it hard to believe that a Fiero will float.  If the air intake silencer was removed (in the left quarter panel) I can easily imagine rust in the intake. <P>As far as the brakes: I just overhauled my brakes. I am impressed with how quickly and securely they stop a 2500 lb car.<P>I was convinced to get a Fiero after being impressed by a Friend's V-8 Fiero that had a v-8 in it. His used the standard brakes and has for almost 8 years.  Stops somewhere around 140 to 150 feet from 60 mph. But I do admit that both of us are over 40 and we probably drive a little differently  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I use the brakes seldom, as I downshift more than others and I also drive "ahead". <P> 150 feet 60 to zero is rapid enough for me ... but ya know, I've heard Porche owners who claimed that their cars with 12" rotors didn't stop fast enough so ... it may be more perception than not. <P> <p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spektyr||04-22-2001||04:15 PM||||A Fiero might float in Mercury, but not water.  My friend just thought he was floating, probably just had enough water going under the front to lift the tires and eliminate his ability to steer.<P>As for the brakes, it isn't so much the stopping ability that bothers me as how hard I have to stand on them to get it stopped quick.  In my Lincoln, just a little pressure and you're stopping quick.  A little more and the tires are howling.  So my gripe with the Fiero isn't how quick it stops, just the "Am I going to stop in time" panic I get when I'm standing on the pedal and the tires aren't squealing yet.<P>That and really good brakes gives me an excuse to demand the use of seatbelts in my car.<P>"Put it on or you'll get a good view of my fascia emblem when I stop.  You'll have to pay for the windshield if you go throught it, too."<P>What I WANT this car to be is a car that out handles it's motor by a noticeable margin, and I WANT a motor pushing 275hp.  Now I may not get what I want, but I'll get as close to it as I can.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000010||GoCartGuy||04-22-2001||09:05 PM||||Just out of curiosity, does your Lincoln have power assist brakes?  Its more than likely your Fiero doesn't.  Took me a little while to get used to that.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ed||04-22-2001||09:17 PM||||Fiero's do have power brakes. <P>As in the other thread, I've never felt under-braked (broke?).  Again, on the track, they grabbed just fine.  I could still lock them up after a couple of hard sessions. And I agree, look at a Porsche magazine and all the brake adds.  <P>Maybe your brakes need service.  <P>And what kind of tires are you running?  After I got rid of the terrible Goodyears, I've no trouble in the rain.  Snow, however, is something else.  <P>Remember, though, where you're headed.  If you want a 275 hp, rear-engined car, with a weight distribution of 40-60 or so with a heavy engine in the rear, or even a lighter version turboed, you WILL need to pay attention to all vehicular inputs.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fldevel||04-22-2001||09:30 PM||||silly answer but .. check oil in tranny 1st and 3rd seem to be the first gears to be affected by low tranny oil ..i know this cause i used to have a small crack in my tranny before i had it replaced and i would know when it was low cause it would be verry difficult to get into 1st and 3rd ..just a thaught ||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||04-22-2001||11:35 PM||||Regarding the rust in your air filter canister...<P>Out here in California we barely have weather much less Kansas sized puddles but I noticed a little bit of rust where the filter sits. I have replaced my air box with a straight tube and have driven in heavy rain on the freeway with no driving problems. But even out here on the Left Coast I have experienced just a little rust and debris in the canister.||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||04-23-2001||09:06 AM||||Funny you should mention brakes - I ended up doing mine this weekend. A common problem with GM's rear calipers, especially aluminum ones, is that the auto-adjust and then the piston freezes over time. Before I started I had a semi-mushy pedal and above average effort to stop. After taking things apart, I found the right rear caliper completely dead and the left almost there. So in addition to OK fronts I had about 20% on the left rear and 0% on the right (explaining a left hand pull). I got premium NAPA pads for all 4 and some reman rear calipers ($75/ea at NAPA - thank goodness I live 15 min away from a regional warehouse!!). After a few dozen miles and manually adjusting the rears up (my front park brake cable is stuck & not working) I now have a good pedal and don't have to stand on the brakes to make an easy stop.||||reg||1
